For the normalization in <a href="#eq:collision" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:collision">[eq:collision]</a>, the symmetric bilinear gain is $$\label{ker:carleman}
 P(c,h)(v)
 =2\int_{\mathbb R^3}c(p)\,\,\mathrm dp
       \int_{\mathbb R^3}
       \delta\bigl((v-p)\cdot(\xi-v)\bigr)h(\xi)\,\,\mathrm d\xi.$$ For fixed $p\ne v$, the inner delta denotes surface measure on the plane $(v-p)\cdot(\xi-v)=0$, divided by $|v-p|$. For incoming velocities $p,\xi$, the outgoing measure has total mass $2\pi|p-\xi|$ and is uniform on the sphere with diameter $[p,\xi]$. Formula <a href="#ker:carleman" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ker:carleman">[ker:carleman]</a> also defines the gain when $c$ is a nonnegative velocity measure and $h$ is a nonnegative density, whenever the displayed integral is finite almost everywhere.

Let $\lambda$ be a probability mixture of the measures $\mathcal U_d$, $d>0$, and $\delta_0$, and let $\rho$ be any axial probability measure. No bound on its density or its support is required. Put $c=\lambda\otimes\rho$. For $R\geq1$, $\bar v\ne0$, and an interval $I\subset\mathbb R$ of length $0<\ell\leq2R$, one has $$\begin{aligned}
 &\frac{1}{W(v)}\int c(\,\mathrm dp)
   \int_{|\xi-v|\leq R}
    \delta\bigl((v-p)\cdot(\xi-v)\bigr)W(\xi)\,\,\mathrm d\xi
     \leq C_q R^2,\label{ker:geometric-total}\\
 &\frac{1}{W(v)}\int c(\,\mathrm dp)
   \int_{\substack{|\xi-v|\leq R\\ \xi_3\in I}}
    \delta\bigl((v-p)\cdot(\xi-v)\bigr)W(\xi)\,\,\mathrm d\xi
     \leq C_q\bigl(R\ell+R^{q-1}\ell^{2-q}\bigr).
     \label{ker:geometric-interval}
\end{aligned}$$ The constants are independent of all transverse widths and of $\rho$. In particular, these statements hold with $\lambda=\mathcal G_d$ for every $d\geq0$.

For every $L<\infty$ there is $C_{q,m,L}<\infty$ such that $$\label{ker:sheet-gain}
 P(c,H)(v)\leq C_{q,m,L}H(v)
 \quad\hbox{for almost every }v,
 \qquad c\in\mathcal S_L.$$ More quantitatively, for $R\geq1$, $$\label{ker:shell-tail}
 \mathcal K_c\bigl(v;
      R<|p|+|\xi-v|\leq2R\bigr)
 \leq C_{q,m,L}R^2e^{-cR^2},$$ where $c>0$ is an absolute constant. The same conclusions, multiplied by $A_0$, hold under sheet domination with amplitude $A_0$.

Fix $L<\infty$. The following conclusions hold uniformly for $c\in\mathcal S_L$ and for outgoing velocities off the axis.*



1.  *The tails in $|p|+|\xi-v|$ are uniformly negligible: $$\label{ker:uniform-tail}
     \lim_{R\to\infty}\sup_{c\in\mathcal S_L}\mathop{\mathrm{ess\,sup}}_v
     \mathcal K_c(v;|p|+|\xi-v|>R)=0.$$ Also, $$\label{ker:large-output}
     \lim_{M\to\infty}\sup_{c\in\mathcal S_L}
     \mathop{\mathrm{ess\,sup}}_{|v|>M}\mathcal K_c(v;\mathbb R^3\times\mathbb R^3)=0.$$*



2.  *For every fixed $M,\delta>0$, $$\begin{aligned}
     &\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}
     \sup_{c\in\mathcal S_L}
     \mathop{\mathrm{ess\,sup}}_{\substack{|v|\leq M\\0<|\bar v|\leq\varepsilon}}
     \mathcal K_c(v;|\bar\xi|\geq\delta)=0,
     \label{ker:axis-input}\\
     &\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}
     \sup_{c\in\mathcal S_L}
     \mathop{\mathrm{ess\,sup}}_{\substack{|v|\leq M\\0<|\bar v|\leq\varepsilon}}
     \mathcal K_c(v;|v_3-p_3|\geq\delta)=0.
     \label{ker:axis-mismatch}
    \end{aligned}$$*



3.  *The axial input marginals are uniformly non-atomic. More precisely, for each $R\geq1$ and each interval $I$ of length $0<\ell\leq1$, $$\label{ker:axial-interval}
     \mathcal K_c(v;|p|+|\xi-v|\leq R,\ \xi_3\in I)
     \leq C_{q,m,L,R}\bigl(\ell+\ell^{2-q}\bigr).$$ In combination with <a href="#ker:uniform-tail" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ker:uniform-tail">[ker:uniform-tail]</a>, this gives $$\label{ker:axial-modulus}
     \lim_{\ell\downarrow0}\sup_{c\in\mathcal S_L}\mathop{\mathrm{ess\,sup}}_v
     \sup_{|I|\leq\ell}\mathcal K_c(v;\xi_3\in I)=0.$$*


4.  *Let $\mathcal S_L(\varepsilon)$ denote the subclass whose elementary transverse widths are at most $\varepsilon$. For every $M<\infty$ and $r_0>0$, $$\label{ker:off-axis-small-input}
     \lim_{\delta\downarrow0}\limsup_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}
     \sup_{c\in\mathcal S_L(\varepsilon)}
     \mathop{\mathrm{ess\,sup}}_{\substack{|v|\leq M\\|\bar v|\geq r_0}}
     \mathcal K_c(v;|\bar\xi|<\delta)=0.$$*

*All these conclusions hold under uniformly bounded sheet amplitudes.

Let a measure in parameters $w=(\bar w,w_3)\in\mathbb R^2\times\mathbb R$ have density $j$ satisfying $$0\leq j(w)\leq C_0\pi^{-3/2}e^{-|w|^2}.$$ Let its velocity image be given by $$\label{ker:parameter-map}
 \bar p=d\bar w,\qquad p_3=\Phi(w_3),\qquad
 0\leq d\leq\tfrac1{16},\qquad
 |\Phi(a)|\leq A+\delta|a|,\quad0\leq\delta\leq\tfrac1{16},$$ where $\Phi$ is merely measurable. The image measure is dominated by $C_0$ times a sheet in $\mathcal S_{L_A}$, with $L_A$ depending only on $A$. Its gain satisfies Lemma <a href="#lem:sheet-bound" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:sheet-bound">6</a> with a constant depending only on $q,m,A,C_0$.*


*If $c_{>B}$ is the image of the restricted parameter measure $\mathbf 1_{|w|>B}j(w)\,\,\mathrm dw$, then $$\label{ker:parameter-tail}
 \mathop{\mathrm{ess\,sup}}_v\frac{P(c_{>B},H)(v)}{H(v)}
 \leq C_{q,m,A,C_0}e^{-B^2/2},\qquad B\geq0.$$ Thus parameter truncations are valid in the normalized collision measure, even when $\Phi$ compresses intervals or has atoms in its image law. Probability mixtures of measurable families with common $q,m,A,C_0$ and the width bounds in <a href="#ker:parameter-map" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ker:parameter-map">[ker:parameter-map]</a> preserve these assertions. More generally, a positive mixing measure of total mass $B_0<\infty$ multiplies the domination amplitude and quantitative upper bounds by $B_0$; the sheet parameter $L_A$ is unchanged. For <a href="#ker:parameter-tail" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ker:parameter-tail">[ker:parameter-tail]</a>, impose the restriction $|w|>B$ in each component before mixing. Additional measurable masks taking values in $[0,1]$ preserve the assertions.

There is a constant $C_{q,m}$ such that $$\label{ker:hot-gain}
 P(H,H)\leq C_{q,m}H
 \quad\hbox{almost everywhere}.$$ In fact the proof below works for every $m>5$.

Every sequence <a href="#eq:rescaling" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:rescaling">[eq:rescaling]</a> has a subsequence for which $h_{r_N}\rightharpoonup g$ in local spacetime $L^1$ on $(0,U)\times\mathbb R^3_x\times\mathbb R^3_v$, where $$\label{eq:limit-equation}
0\leq g(t,x,v)\leq(t+l)H(v),\qquad
D_tg=2P(B,g)-g\nu_B.$$ Here $B$ is <a href="#eq:background" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:background">[eq:background]</a>; its gain and loss are defined by integration against the velocity measure. Both terms on the right are locally integrable, with a velocity majorant uniform in position on each compact positive-time interval. Compactly supported velocity averages of $h_{r_N}$ converge strongly in local $L^1_{t,x}$. The limit has a locally strongly continuous $L^1$ representative at positive times and obeys the integrating-factor formula <a href="#dil:mild" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="dil:mild">[dil:mild]</a> below.*


*If $l>0$, pass also to a limit $\lambda_N\to\lambda_0$ of the shot parameters. The conclusion and its local continuity extend to time zero, with $$\label{dil:initial-trace}
g(0,x,v)=\lambda_0 l\,\chi(x/l,v).$$

There are nonnegative velocity measures $A_n(t,x,\,\mathrm dp)$ and numbers $\varepsilon_n\to0$ with the following properties. After discarding finitely many terms so that $r_n\le\overline T$, the measures $A_n$ are defined from free transport on the whole interval $0\le t\le1$, independently of the cap shot’s existence interval. The bounds on $A_n$ below hold on that whole interval; comparison with $h_n$ is used only while the shot exists.*


1.  *On $0<t<\min(1,U_n)$, along almost every free characteristic, $$\label{nc:positive-equation}
     D_th_n\le 2P(A_n,h_n)+\varepsilon_n H .$$ At each point $A_n$ contains at most one main free core. Its dilated scale $\mu=s/r_n$ satisfies $|y|\asymp\mu$, with coordinates measured from its periodic copy, and $$\frac{2P(A_n,H)}{H}\le\frac{C}{t+\ell_n}.$$*


2.  *Before spatial masks are imposed, a core of scale $\mu=s/r_n$ and sign $\sigma$ is the pushforward of $$\label{nc:core-parameters}
     \frac{K S\bigl(\gamma(z-tp_3)/\mu\bigr)}
          {\mu+t\alpha\gamma S\bigl(\gamma(z-tp_3)/\mu\bigr)}
     J(w)\,\mathrm dw ,
     \qquad
     \bar p=d_s\bar w,\quad
     p_3=\sigma+\alpha\tanh\bigl(\gamma(z-tp_3)/\mu\bigr)+d_sw_3 .$$ The actual masks are measurable factors between zero and one. The density in <a href="#nc:core-parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="nc:core-parameters">[nc:core-parameters]</a> is at most $C(t+\mu)^{-1}J(w)$.*


3.  *For $t\ge a>0$, the normalized gain measure $$\label{nc:normalized-kernel}
     \mathcal K_n(t,x,v;\,\mathrm dp\,\,\mathrm d\xi)
     =
     4A_n(t,x,\,\mathrm dp)\,
     \delta\bigl((v-p)\cdot(\xi-v)\bigr)
     \frac{H(\xi)}{H(v)}\,\mathrm d\xi$$ has total mass at most $C/a$. Uniformly in its base point, the contributions from large $|p|+|\xi-v|$, or from large retained Gaussian parameters in a product-sheet majorant, tend to zero. Its total mass tends uniformly to zero as $|v|\to\infty$. The axial interval, axial mismatch, and small transverse input conclusions of Lemma <a href="#lem:kernel-tightness" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:kernel-tightness">7</a> apply to $\mathcal K_n$, with the amplitude factor $C/a$.

On every compact set with $t\ge a>0$, the following statements hold uniformly.*


1.  *If $a\le t_2\le t_1$, $\Delta=t_1-t_2$, and $z_2=z_1-\Delta w$, then $$\label{nc:matching-inequality}
     |w-u_{\mu,\sigma}(t_1,z_1)|
     \le |w-u_{\mu,\sigma}(t_2,z_2)|.$$*


2.  *As $\mu\downarrow0$, the distance of $u_{\mu,\sigma}(t,z)$ to $$\{z/t,\ \sigma-\alpha,\ \sigma+\alpha\}$$ tends to zero uniformly. For the perturbed axial velocity in <a href="#nc:core-parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="nc:core-parameters">[nc:core-parameters]</a>, $$\label{nc:thermal-speed-error}
     |p_3-u_{\mu,\sigma}(t,z)|\le d_s|w_3|.$$

Let $n\ne0$, $e=n/|n|$, and let $\zeta$ be a bounded nonnegative function supported in $[-1,1]$ with integral one. For every nonnegative measurable $F$, $$\label{nc:plane-identity}
 \int_{n^\perp} F(k)\frac{\,\mathrm dA(k)}{|n|}
 =
 \int_{\mathbb R^3}F(n\times L)|n|\zeta(L\cdot e)\,\mathrm dL .$$ If $\delta\le|n|\le R$ and $|k|\le R_1$, the right side is supported in $$|L|\le\sqrt{(R_1/\delta)^2+1}$$ and has density at most $R\|\zeta\|_\infty$. The identity can be applied successively when each normal is a measurable function of parameters chosen at earlier gains.

For these fixed $\kappa,L$, there are $J\in\mathbb N$ and $\varepsilon_*\in(0,1/4)$ with the following property. Suppose a measurable medium obeys, throughout every speed-$D$ excursion from a specified starting point over a duration $A\in I_L$, $$\label{br:medium-closeness}
|b_\sigma-1|\le\varepsilon_*,
\qquad |w_\sigma-\sigma e_3|\le\varepsilon_*
\quad(\sigma=\pm1).$$ From each starting velocity in $E$, histories with at most $J$ collisions, speed at most $D$ on every free flight, every postcollision velocity and the final velocity strictly inside $E$, and $$\label{br:robust-drift}
|Z_A-\kappa Ae_1|<d\kappa A$$ have total weight greater than 3. The initial free flight may have speed $D$. No continuity of the medium is needed.
